<h2>A Work That Says What Words Cannot</h2>
<p><em>Finding a value in a work of art you already know</em></p>
<p>This unit claims that the arts do something no other field of study does: they do not just talk about values, they make values visible, audible, or tangible. A painting does not argue that war is terrible; it shows you what the terror looks like. A song does not explain grief; it lets you feel it. This reflection asks you to test that claim on a work you already know.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>The prompt</strong></p>
<p>Choose a work of art that has mattered to you. It can be a song, a film, a painting or photograph, a building, a poem, a dance, or anything else that counts as art. It should be a work you have <strong>actually experienced</strong>, not one you have only heard about.</p>
<p>In your reflection, do the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>Name the work and describe, in specific terms, one moment or detail that affected you.</li>
<li>Name the value the work makes visible (for example justice, grief, freedom, loyalty, fear, wonder) and say why you chose that word.</li>
<li>Explain how the work's <em>form</em> (its structure, not just its subject) makes that value hard to ignore. Could a plain statement of the same idea do the same job? Why or why not?</li>
</ol>
</div></div>
<p>The third part matters most. It is easy to say a song is sad or a painting is powerful. The work of this course is saying <em>what the work does</em>, in particular terms, to make you feel it. If you cannot find a difference between what the work shows and what a sentence could say, you may have found the exception that tests the rule. That is a good result too.</p>
<p>If you need a starting point, look back at the Gallery for this unit and choose the work you reacted to most strongly.</p>
